NAME
       grep, egrep, fgrep - print lines that match a pattern

SYNOPSIS
       grep [OPTION...] PATTERN [FILE...]
       grep [OPTION...] -e PATTERN ... [FILE...]
       grep [OPTION...] -f PATTERN-FILE [FILE...]

DESCRIPTION
       grep reads each FILE (standard input when there is none, or when FILE
       is -) and prints the lines that contain a match for PATTERN. When more
       than one file is searched, each line starts with the file's name.

       By default PATTERN is a basic regular expression (BRE). In a BRE,
       . * [ ] ^ and $ are special; to use ? + { | ( and ) as operators you
       must put a backslash before them. With -E the pattern is an extended
       regular expression (ERE), where those characters work without the
       backslash. With -F the pattern is a plain string.

       egrep and fgrep are old names for grep -E and grep -F. They still work,
       but scripts should use grep -E and grep -F.

       Exit status: 0 when a line matched, 1 when nothing matched, 2 when an
       error happened (for example a missing file). That makes grep -q handy
       in if statements.

   Regular expression reminders
       .        any one character
       [abc]    one of a, b or c; [^abc] any character except those
       [0-9]    a digit; [[:digit:]], [[:alpha:]], [[:space:]] also work
       ^  $     the start and the end of the line
       *        the item before, repeated zero or more times
       +  ?     (ERE) one or more times, zero or one time
       {N,M}    (ERE) between N and M times
       a|b      (ERE) a or b
       ( )      (ERE) a group
       \<  \>   the start and the end of a word

OPTIONS
       -i, --ignore-case
              Ignore the difference between upper and lower case.

       -v, --invert-match
              Print the lines that do NOT match.

       -c, --count
              Print only the number of matching lines.

       -l, --files-with-matches
              Print only the names of files that contain a match.

       -L, --files-without-match
              Print only the names of files with no match.

       -n, --line-number
              Put the line number before each line.

       -w, --word-regexp
              Match whole words only.

       -x, --line-regexp
              The whole line must match.

       -o, --only-matching
              Print only the matching part of the line, one per line.

       -q, --quiet, --silent
              Print nothing; only set the exit status.

       -s, --no-messages
              Do not report missing or unreadable files.

       -r, --recursive
              Search every file under each directory given.

       -R, --dereference-recursive
              Like -r, and follow symbolic links.

       -h, --no-filename
              Do not print file names before the lines.

       -H, --with-filename
              Always print file names.

       -E, --extended-regexp
              PATTERN is an extended regular expression.

       -F, --fixed-strings
              PATTERN is a plain string, not a regular expression.

       -e PATTERN, --regexp=PATTERN
              Use PATTERN. Give -e more than once to match any of several
              patterns, or to use a pattern that starts with -.

       -f FILE, --file=FILE
              Read patterns from FILE, one per line.

       -A N, -B N, -C N
              Also print N lines after (-A), before (-B) or around (-C) each
              match.

       -m N, --max-count=N
              Stop after N matching lines.

       --include=GLOB, --exclude=GLOB
              With -r, search only (or skip) files whose names match GLOB.

       --color=auto
              Highlight matches on a terminal. RHEL 9 sets an alias so that
              grep does this by default in interactive shells.

EXAMPLES
       Show the line for the user student in /etc/passwd:

           grep '^student:' /etc/passwd

       Save every line of a word list that contains "ich" to a file:

           grep ich /usr/share/dict/words > /root/lines.txt

       Show a configuration file without comments and empty lines:

           grep -v -e '^#' -e '^$' /etc/chrony.conf

       The same with one extended expression:

           grep -Ev '^(#|$)' /etc/ssh/sshd_config

       List the files under /etc that mention lab.example.com:

           grep -rl lab.example.com /etc

       Use grep in a script test:

           if grep -q '^student:' /etc/passwd; then echo "student exists"; fi

SEE ALSO
       sed(1), awk(1), find(1), regex(7)
